letter -> refuse, no letter maybe irrelevant; choose because predictor won't send if policy refuses. Need maybe one example with multi-agent where policy vs action matters: a predictor rewards you iff your policy is one-box-full? Hmm action FDT can handle by intervening on action at observation. Policy difference maybe in a game where another player observes your policy before acting, not your action; or where you face multiple possible observations and other player predicts your whole policy. Need develop a simple example: \"Policy Predictor\": Predictor examines your decision policy, not just what you'll do in actual situation. If it predicts your policy is generous in both branches, gives reward before observation; if not, no reward. Action-based FDT that intervenes separately at actual observation might fail? Let's think. Suppose agent will see a red or blue light randomly; then choose pay/keep. Predictor before light predicts entire policy. Pays $1M if policy is pay-on-red-and-pay-on-blue? But actual observation may be red; action FDT at red might choose pay if predictor's reward depends on action? But if reward depends on whole policy, need choose policy. Maybe source footnote says difference only matters in multi-agent dilemmas. Could create: two agents, each chooses policy; their payoffs depend on policies being identical? Hmm. Need maybe explain without detailed example? But prompt says use examples whenever help. Need include one example where policy selection matters. Could use \"policy twin\": twin chooses policy before knowing observation; your policy and twin's policy must match? Maybe. Need careful not invent too much but can propose. Need distinguish source vs own. Could say source does not specify multi-agent cases; under natural operationalization, P-FDT evaluates whole policy. Example: two agents separately asked to choose a policy for a later coin flip; if both policies output same action after coin, both get reward. P-FDT chooses policy assuming